To help you meet your deadline, here’s a concise, implementation-ready spec your Privacy/Compliance–approved reporting/EHR analytics team can use to generate exactly what you requested from active MedicationRequest data.

Retrieval (minimum necessary)
- Query: GET /MedicationRequest?patient=Patient/S1733937&status=active
- Limit returned elements if your FHIR server supports _elements (e.g., medicationCodeableConcept, subject, status, authoredOn, dosageInstruction, medicationReference).

Field extraction and normalization
- Medication name/strength:
  - Prefer medicationCodeableConcept.text; fallback to coding.display.
  - If a Medication resource is referenced, use Medication.code and Medication.form if available.
- Route:
  - dosageInstruction[0].route.coding/display or text (normalize synonyms: PO/by mouth/oral; inhalation; topical; ophthalmic; otic; nasal; rectal; sublingual; buccal; transdermal; injection).
- Frequency (per day):
  - From dosageInstruction[0].timing.repeat:
    - If periodUnit = d: doses/day = frequency (use frequencyMax if present to show a range).
    - If periodUnit = h: doses/day = (24 / period) × frequency.
    - If periodUnit = wk: doses/day = (frequency / period) ÷ 7.
    - If periodUnit = mo: doses/day = (frequency / period) ÷ 30.
  - Handle PRN: if asNeeded[x] true and no frequencyMax, exclude from baseline pill count and mark “PRN”; if frequencyMax present, show a range (baseline–max).
- Formulation:
  - Parse “tablet,” “tab,” “capsule,” “cap,” “inhaler,” “patch,” “solution,” “suspension,” etc., from Medication.code/Medication.form or medicationCodeableConcept.text.
- Ingredient (for grouping and duplicate checks):
  - Prefer ingredient-level codes (e.g., RxNorm ingredient) if present.
  - Else normalize the generic name by stripping strength/form (e.g., “lisinopril 10 mg tablet” → “lisinopril”).
  - For combination products, split components if ingredient codings available; otherwise treat as its own combo unless you can reliably map components.

Grouping, pill burden, and duplicate therapy logic
- Grouping key: normalized ingredient + normalized formulation + normalized route.
- Daily oral pill burden:
  - Include only items with oral route AND formulation of tablet or capsule.
  - Count “doses per day” as calculated above. If ranges, maintain a min–max; if PRN without max, exclude from the total and list separately as PRN.
  - Sum across all qualifying items → Total daily oral pill count (report integer or range).
- Non-oral list:
  - Everything else (inhalers, injectables, ophthalmic/otic, nasal, topical, transdermal patches, rectal, buccal/sublingual, nebulized, etc.).
- Potential duplicate therapy flags:
  - Flag when two or more active orders share the same normalized ingredient and route (e.g., “atorvastatin” oral tab vs brand-equivalent).
  - Also flag if a single-ingredient order coexists with a combination product containing that ingredient (if component mapping is available).

Suggested output structure
- Oral tablets/capsules (grouped by ingredient + formulation + route)
  - Ingredient: [name]
    - [Medication name] [strength], [route], [frequency text] — [tablet/capsule]
    - …
- Non-oral items
  - [Medication name] [strength], [route], [frequency text] — [form]
- Potential duplicate therapies (shared ingredient)
  - [Ingredient]: [Medication A], [Medication B], …
- Total daily oral pill count: [N] or [N–M]
  - Notes: PRN handling, any assumptions or unknowns.

Edge cases to handle
- Multiple dosageInstruction entries: prefer the most recent or status=active instruction per local rules.
- Taper or titration schedules: if timing cannot be converted reliably, list as “variable dosing” and exclude from the total; add a note.
- Non-discrete frequencies (e.g., “qod,” “twice weekly”): convert using the same rules (every other day = 0.5/day; twice weekly ≈ 0.2857/day).
- Duplicate orders with different strengths but same ingredient: flag as potential duplicate unless clearly intentional (document uncertainty).

Optional PR-ready summary phrasing (template)
- “[First name] takes a total of [N] oral pills per day, including [key ingredient groups, e.g., ‘one statin tablet and one blood-pressure tablet’]. Non-oral therapies include [list by form, e.g., ‘a weekly patch and a daily inhaler’].”

