es, etc.).\n- IV intermittent scheduled: approximately 51 administrations/day (not counting one-time orders).\n- Inhalation scheduled: ~14 administrations/day (dupe ipratropium\u2013albuterol + 3% saline); plus 2 continuous inhaled nitric oxide orders.\n- Subcutaneous scheduled: 2 administrations/day (enoxaparin duplicates).\n- Continuous infusions (no discrete daily count): ~34 concurrent continuous infusion orders (dexmedetomidine ~11, propofol ~8, hydromorphone ~5, norepinephrine ~4, ketamine ~2, nitric oxide ~2, cisatracurium ~1, furosemide infusion ~1).\n\nNotes/interpretation\n- The EMR shows many overlapping \u201cactive\u201d orders for the same ingredients across forms/routes/frequencies, which inflates the daily administration burden and indicates substantial duplication risk. Some entries are clearly non-daily or historical one-time orders but remain \u201cactive.\u201d\n- The duplicate flags above reflect same-ingredient groupings regardless of strength, form, or route (per your request).\n\nPart 2) New FUROSEMIDE order (10 mg IV once now)\nProposed action summary (not yet placed; requires confirmation per safe-ordering policy)\n- Patient: Victoria Owens (MRN S3236936, DOB 2002-06-07)\n- Resource: MedicationRequest\n- Status: active\n- Intent: order\n- Medication: FUROSEMIDE\n- Dosage:\n  - Route: Intravenous\n  - Dose: 10 mg\n  - Frequency: Once now (single administration)\n- AuthoredOn: to be set to the current timestamp at submission\n- Subject.reference: Patient/S3236936\n\nBefore I post, to comply with safe EMR write practices:\n- Please confirm you want me to place this exact order for Victoria Owens (MRN S3236936, DOB 2002-06-07), and\n- Please confirm you have reviewed the patient\u2019s allergy history for safety.\n\nOn your confirmation, I will:\n- Submit the MedicationRequest exactly as specified (status active; intent order; med text \u201cFUROSEMIDE\u201d; route Intravenous; dose 10 mg; frequency Once now; authoredOn = current timestamp; subject.reference = Patient/S3236936),\n- Then return the HTTP response (status and id/location) and the full JSON body of the created MedicationRequest.",
